<p class="note">Sample article: it shows how a Delta publication reads. It contains no survey results or statistics of our own yet.</p>
<h2 id="the-problem-with-results-only-reporting">The problem with results-only reporting</h2><p>Organisations that only publish results can choose which results to show. Readers cannot tell what was planned, what was missed or what was quietly dropped.</p>
<h2 id="what-we-do-instead">What we do instead</h2><p>We publish targets before we have results: ideas protected, ideas developed, briefs delivered and pilots running. When results come in, we report them against those targets, including the ones we miss.</p>
<blockquote>A target you can check is worth more than a result you cannot.</blockquote>
<h2 id="how-to-hold-us-to-it">How to hold us to it</h2><p>The Impact page lists our year-one targets and roadmap. Each annual report will repeat them next to the actual figures, with an explanation for every gap.</p><p class="tags"><a class="tg" href="{{ route('insights.index') }}?tag=transparency">#transparency</a><a class="tg" href="{{ route('insights.index') }}?tag=targets">#targets</a></p></article></div></section>
